WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.343
- I love my brother.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:06.190
But Ted Kaczynski's
brother loved him, too.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:07.890
- Oh, what you thought that witnesses

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.343
just showed up at trial having

00:00:09.343 --> 00:00:11.940
never practiced their testimony before?

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.219
Oh, you sweet, sweet summer child.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:16.802
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.160
So, I have a confession to make.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.660
This is not the first time I've seen

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.180
this episode of "Better call
Saul!" called "Chicanery".

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.010
It's the episode that involves
a bar disciplinary trial.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.000
And I saw this episode about a year ago,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.833
and ever since then,

00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:31.820
I haven't been able to
think about anything else.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.440
If you've seen it, you know that there

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.810
are questions about whether
what Jimmy did is legal,

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:36.860
and not only whether it's legal,

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.130
but also whether the things that

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.820
he does would actually work

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.630
in this bar disciplinary hearing,

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.330
and whether the evidence that he generates

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:44.750
would be admissible in court.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.982
I think I finally know
how everything connects

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:49.130
and whether it's accurate or not.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.630
So, let's dig in to "Better
Call Saul!", "Chicanery".

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.020
- Charles thinks that Jimmy somehow

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.500
took control of your documents while

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.740
he was working on them at his home.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.770
He believes Jimmy transposed
the address numbers.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.210
- Transposed the numbers?

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.723
How would he even do that?

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.430
- Charles contends that Jimmy took

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.350
the documents pertaining
to the Rosella branch,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.003
and while Charles was indisposed,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.600
photocopied and doctored them.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.840
- That's pretty Baroque.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.040
- Okay, so this is the
background to the episode,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.020
and it is true that under the
rules of professional conduct,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.950
if a lawyer realizes that a
document has been fabricated,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.690
they have an obligation to
tell the tribunal or the court

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.870
that it has been doctored,
and come forward with that.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.820
Now, it's also just
good client management.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.330
Kim is trying to do the right thing here.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.610
This is a PR issue for the client,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.060
and it might reflect poorly
on the client itself.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.820
- [Mr. Alley] Okay, this is it.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.490
We can run without lights and mics.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.600
We'll collect all cell phones

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.670
and hold them for the
duration of your testimony.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.210
- Okay, so a reminder here,
this is not a normal trial.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.370
This is a disciplinary hearing by the bar,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.040
so it's a question of whether Jimmy

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.320
gets to keep his bar license.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.654
And of course his brother, Chuck,

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.760
claims to be allergic to electricity

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.120
so they're turning off all
the lights to accommodate him.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.611
This is probably going to
be more like an arbitration,

00:02:08.611 --> 00:02:12.210
less formal, but still
has the force of law here.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.640
But look, I'm making assumptions here.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.060
I don't know exactly what
the state bar of New Mexico

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.790
would be doing under these circumstances.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.730
- How are you feeling, Chuck?

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:21.900
- I'll muddle through.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.450
- Because this is a lot to ask,

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:24.283
and if you're not up to it,

00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:26.280
just throwing this out there,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.020
maybe you don't need to testify at all.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:28.930
- No, I do.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.930
I'm the only person who can adequately

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.820
explain the context of that tape.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.190
Otherwise, the defense
will tear it to shreds.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.000
- It's already a solid case.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.360
We have Jimmy's statement from

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.610
the pre-prosecution diversion.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.760
There's my testimony
and the private eye's.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.770
Maybe there's no need to put you through

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:45.920
the ringer like this.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.220
- Okay, so a couple of things here.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.320
Number one, the tape here is that

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.200
Chuck recorded Jimmy basically confessing

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.490
to the crimes at issue here
of doctoring the evidence.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:56.970
So, it shows one of the reasons

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.160
why Jimmy should be disbarred.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:02:59.280
But another thing that's going here

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.610
is something that does actually happen,

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.400
which is that you can often
gild the lily in trial.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.580
Some witnesses are going
to be better than others.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.100
And if you already have a rock solid case,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.380
and you have a witness who
doesn't play well on the stand,

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.310
or there are other issues that are going

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.370
to confuse the jury or the judge,

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:20.010
then it's best not to
put that witness forward

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.630
because it's just going to detract

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.200
from your already strong case.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.260
And of course, Chuck,
isn't listening to that,

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.090
and I don't think his law
partner can stop him here,

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.530
but we know that this
is also a foreshadowing

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.750
that he is not the best
witness to tell the story

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.520
and bad things are going to happen

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.210
because he refuses to not take the stand.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.940
- [Mr. Alley] In the
matter of James M. McGill,

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.890
the state bar intends
to prove the following

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.803
violations of the ethical code.

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:50.734
16-102, engaging in conduct
the lawyer knows is criminal.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.420
Mr. McGill certainly
knew that breaking down

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.563
his brother's door was criminal behavior.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.370
16-804, committing a criminal act that

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.820
reflects adversely on the lawyer's honesty

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.030
or trustworthiness as a lawyer.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.670
Any reasonable person would agree that

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.750
assaulting another lawyer in his own home

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:12.320
reflects badly on Mr. McGill's
fitness as an attorney.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.048
- I think those are the actual

00:04:14.048 --> 00:04:15.970
rules of professional conduct.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.580
I think that is also
the case in New Mexico.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:21.168
Certainly, the section numbers correspond

00:04:21.168 --> 00:04:24.160
with the ABA model professional codes.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.120
Not surprisingly, lawyers cannot engage

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.750
in manifestly illegal conduct,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.769
and they can't engage in conduct that

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.810
reflects on the honesty of
themselves and the profession.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.850
So, I think those are the actual things

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.210
that Jimmy would be charged with

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.690
in exactly this circumstance.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.680
That's great attention
to detail right there.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.710
- Mr. McGill broke into his brother's home

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:43.590
and destroyed an audio cassette

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.870
which contained a
recording of a conversation

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.720
between himself and his
brother, Charles McGill.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.010
We will show that this recording was

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.490
evidence in an ongoing legal case.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.680
The state bar believes the once
we have presented the facts,

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.860
the committee will agree that disbarment

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.663
is warranted for James McGill.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:02.470
Thank you very much.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.780
- Okay, so assuming that this is

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.300
an opening statement in a trial,

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.670
and yeah, there are
going to be differences

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.730
given that this is a bar
disciplinary hearing.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:10.840
I would have liked to have heard

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.907
more about the facts that the state,

00:05:13.907 --> 00:05:14.740
or the state bar, is going to prove here.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:15.870
They focused on the law here,

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:19.067
that is relevant, and that
law appears to be spot on,

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.070
and probably makes sense
in a disciplinary hearing,

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:21.940
but at the same time,

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:24.130
he is using the language
of an opening statement

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.180
that they are going to prove these things

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:27.810
that we're not arguing anything here.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.970
We're just giving an opening statement

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:32.300
about what the facts and
the law are going to be.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.090
And that's pretty accurate.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.650
- I'm Kim Wexler,
co-counsel with James McGill

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:37.950
for the defense.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.640
You've already read Mr. McGill's
pre-prosecution statement.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.520
We don't dispute he broke
into his brother's house.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.913
An act he regrets deeply,

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:50.420
but there is another side to this story.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:55.193
One not about calculation or ill intent,

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.640
but about two brothers whose relationship

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.610
after years of strain finally broke.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:07.640
We believe that after you
have the complete picture,

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.290
you'll understand James McGill is an asset

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:12.210
to our legal community

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.660
and he should remain a full member

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.160
of it in good standing.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.290
- Okay, another super short, very surgical

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.780
opening statement from Kim here.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:22.450
Again, I would have liked her
to focus on more of the facts.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.170
They've opened the question about

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:25.610
the relationship between the two brothers

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.560
and how that colors this whole thing.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.640
How could this crime be
turned from something

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.690
that's clearly illegal into something that

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.070
is understandable and forgivable

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.590
because of the brothers' relationship?

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.350
Frankly, if Jimmy is on the stand,

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.330
he can lay a lot of that foundation.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.300
- Mr. Alley, call your witness.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.310
- [Howard] Bang! Bang! Bang!

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.570
Then, he kicked down the door.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:46.980
Jimmy was very agitated.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.270
He was shouting.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.880
He demanded Charles turnover the evidence

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:51.713
he'd been collecting-

00:06:51.713 --> 00:06:52.546
- [Kim] Objection!

00:06:52.546 --> 00:06:55.090
We haven't established the
tape is evidence of anything.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.940
The defense has only acknowledged
it as a piece of property.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:59.700
- [Howard] Allow me to rephrase.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.770
- I love that Hamlin is actually adding

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.790
some drama because he is
a seasoned trial lawyer.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.690
So, he does know what works

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:08.470
and what doesn't work on the stand.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.460
This objection that Kim has made,

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.150
basically the judge will
smack that down and say,

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.920
look, I understand that this
is the witness's opinion

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.500
and I'll take it for what it's worth.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.100
And so they're not going to
strike the testimony here.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.030
They're just going to say, be careful,

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.690
try not to editorialize
or opine from the stand.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.880
And that's exactly what
the witness then does.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.440
When you make an objection like this,

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.430
if you're really worried about
the particular testimony,

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:32.670
you have to ask the judge,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.610
or in this case the panel,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.980
to strike that testimony from the record.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:37.970
Otherwise, it stays there,

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.650
and everyone is allowed
to rely on that testimony.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.470
So, anytime you see an objection,

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.340
if they're not getting
rid of the testimony

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:45.951
that shouldn't have come
out in the first place,

00:07:45.951 --> 00:07:48.330
it was a completely ineffectual act.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.340
- [Howard] Jimmy demanded
an audio cassette

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.860
in Charles's possession,

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:52.900
which Jimmy proceeded,

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:56.010
with the help of an iron
fireplace implement,

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.350
to pry from Charles's his desk.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.060
He then broke the cassette into pieces

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.390
and went on to confront his brother.

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:03.223
- [Mr. Alley] What happened after

00:08:03.223 --> 00:08:05.400
the defendant smashed this tape?

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.490
- It's a great direct
examination question.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.820
And on direct, you're not
allowed to ask leading questions.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:10.653
You are on cross.

00:08:10.653 --> 00:08:12.350
And what happened next is perfect.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:13.748
You're just teeing it up for the witness

00:08:13.748 --> 00:08:17.060
to explain away everything
that the witness can say.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.800
And when you have a great
witness like Hamlin here,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.550
just let them do the talking.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:23.930
- What was your opinion of him then?

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:26.950
- I thought he had a lot of get up and go.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:27.810
He was a hard worker.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.860
- You had a nickname for him, didn't you?

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:32.712
- [Howard] Charlie Hustle.

00:08:32.712 --> 00:08:33.620
(chuckles)

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.690
- So interesting Easter egg here.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:36.810
This isn't a legal thing,

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:39.310
but that is the nickname for Pete Rose,

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.250
who was banned from baseball
for betting on games

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:45.440
that he was a player in and a manager of.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.890
It's an interesting nickname
to give Jimmy there.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.000
- Could you speak to the terms

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.500
of Charles's leave of absence?

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:54.140
- You know I can't.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.810
It was an FMLA leave.

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:57.760
Anything more is confidential.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:08:59.260
- Okay, spot on.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:02.060
The FMLA is the Family
and Medical Leave Act

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:04.300
that allows people to take medical leave,

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.500
and the employer, under
most circumstances,

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.320
is required to allow them
to come back to their job.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.270
There's some exceptions.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:13.060
If that job has been completely
removed, for example,

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.310
then there's no obligation to
bring them back to that job.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:16.900
But that is exactly what would happen

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:18.190
in a situation like this.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.930
And the employer is
under an obligation not

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:25.550
to disclose the reason for
the employee taking the leave.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.010
- At this time, the state bar would like

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:29.200
to enter exhibit five into evidence.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:31.310
We asked that Charles
McGill's recording be played.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.940
- I'm respectfully renewing my objection.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.610
The probative value of
playing this exhibit

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:36.340
is outweighed by how prejudicial it is.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.660
- All due respect to Ms. Wexler,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.240
but the state bar feels
this is fundamental to hear.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:41.320
We don't wanna leave any question

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.410
as to the intentions of both parties.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.460
- Ms. Wexler, we already
ruled this was fair game

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.950
when we denied your motion to suppress.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.470
- I'll add the rough and tumble

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.450
of your client's conduct aside,

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:52.840
we need to know whether one lawyer

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:55.420
attempted to tamper
with another's evidence.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:56.390
- Okay, this is great
for a couple of reasons.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:58.730
Number one, the objection
that Kim is making

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.130
is a real objection.

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:01.210
Under the Rules of Evidence,

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:03.840
if a piece of evidence
has a prejudicial value

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.190
that outweighs its probative value.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.190
In other words, how relevant
it is to the proceedings,

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:09.580
then, under certain circumstances,

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.130
the judge or the tribunal can
rule that it's inadmissible

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.650
because it's just say too
inflammatory or too prejudicial,

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:15.970
but what's great about this,

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:18.380
and what you rarely ever see on TV,

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:22.340
is that these issues of
very, very important pieces

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:26.040
of evidence are almost never
dealt with in the trial itself.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.120
There's almost always pre-trial briefing.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:29.950
And here, there was pre-trial briefing.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:30.960
They moved to suppress it.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.200
That motion was denied.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.210
And so, effectively they
already dealt with this issue.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:35.440
And almost always on TV,

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.520
there's this back and forth
between these objections,

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.670
and sometimes it's heard in
the presence of the jury.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:43.100
In reality, in real life,
it's done in a very dry way.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.960
You file a motion to
either admit the evidence,

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.640
or to prevent it from coming in,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.040
the judge rules on it ahead of time.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.410
And you save everyone the heartache

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.750
of going back and forth
on the day of the trial.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:53.583
It's amazing!

00:10:53.583 --> 00:10:55.110
You never see that on TV.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.253
- I love my brother.

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:00.539
But Ted Kaczynski's
brother loved him, too.

00:11:00.539 --> 00:11:02.800
- Oh, what, you thought that witnesses

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.452
just showed up at trial having never

00:11:04.452 --> 00:11:06.860
practiced their testimony before?

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.200
Oh, you sweet, sweet summer child.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.500
- [Jimmy] Kim deserves Mesa
Verde, not you, not HHM!

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.220
She earned it and she needs it!

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.040
I did it to help her,

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.440
but I honestly didn't think
it would hurt you so bad.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:22.607
I thought you'd just say,

00:11:22.607 --> 00:11:24.210
"Oh crap, I made a mistake,"

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.630
and go on with your life
like a normal person,

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:29.520
but all no wishful thinking!

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.720
- All right, so obviously this raises huge

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:34.770
ethical implications for
Kim representing Jimmy here.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.380
I'm gonna assume that representing someone

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:38.320
in a bar disciplinary hearing is basically

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:40.060
an act of a lawyer and they're bound

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:41.310
by the same ethical issues,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:42.930
and she's a material witness here.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.290
She is one of the reasons that this

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.280
whole situation has come to fruition.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:49.910
It's a little crazy for
her to be counsel here,

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.500
despite the fact that she's
a law partner with Jimmy,

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.120
and maybe especially because
they're law partners.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.490
It's pretty improper for her to be counsel

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.760
for Jimmy in this particular situation.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.500
- [Charles] Jimmy, you do realize

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.833
you just confessed to a felony?

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:06.740
- [Jimmy] I guess.

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:09.850
But you feel better, right?

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.843
Besides it's your word against mine.

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:17.450
- So yeah, look, tampering with evidence

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:19.530
to try and obstruct an investigation

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.174
or to interfere with another court case,

00:12:22.174 --> 00:12:24.410
clearly, clearly illegal,

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.930
and New Mexico is a
one-party consent state.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:30.210
So, it's probably okay for Chuck

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:32.590
to have recorded this conversation

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:33.790
in which he is participating.

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:38.042
Bad evidence for Jimmy.

00:12:38.042 --> 00:12:39.150
- Excuse me.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:39.983
- Sorry.

00:12:39.983 --> 00:12:40.816
- That's yours.

00:12:48.683 --> 00:12:49.516
- Huell!

00:12:49.516 --> 00:12:50.349
Everyone loves Huell!

00:12:50.349 --> 00:12:51.350
Huell is the best.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:54.230
So, we know that he has
bumped into Charles.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:56.720
He has, of course, planted something

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.900
on Charles's person that will
become relevant later on.

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:03.680
This is also incredibly
relevant for the crazy legal

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:05.320
analysis that I had to do to figure out

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:06.153
what's going on here.

00:13:06.153 --> 00:13:06.986
Because number one,

00:13:06.986 --> 00:13:09.600
he's made physical contact with Charles,

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.950
which raises the issue of whether that

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:15.300
is its own crime of
assault and or battery,

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.770
and whether planting
something on Chuck's person

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.100
can be admissible evidence.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:21.140
We'll get to that in a second.

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:23.810
But those are the kinds of legal issues

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:25.010
that we have to think about here.

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:28.320
And it's going to spiral
out of control really fast.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:29.960
- I had a suspicion my brother had

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.760
tampered with documents in
a case I was working on.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:34.310
- Now, why would he do that?

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.300
It doesn't sound particularly brotherly.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:37.710
- I believe it was his hope that

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.190
the tainted documents
would cause the client

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:43.220
to become disillusioned
with my representation,

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:45.200
and return to their previous attorney-

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.430
- Object!

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:47.420
Object!

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:48.510
Yeah, you're not going to be allowed

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:51.735
to speculate as to the kind of motive

00:13:51.735 --> 00:13:53.982
or internal thinking that
Jimmy's going on here.

00:13:53.982 --> 00:13:57.800
- [Charles] Which I should note
is precisely what happened.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:13:58.720
- Objection.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:13:59.553
- Finally!

00:13:59.553 --> 00:14:01.410
- I'm not saying Ms. Wexler
knew what Jimmy was doing

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.110
or had any involvement,

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:04.900
I'm only stating that it happened.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.205
- But the witness can't keep testifying,

00:14:07.205 --> 00:14:10.560
if you're objecting to the court here.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.190
This happens a lot in TV.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:13.360
If there's an objection,

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:14.640
the witness has to stop.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.440
And if you win that objection,

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:17.700
you need to go back and say that

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.970
you move that the testimony
be stricken from the record.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:22.330
Otherwise, it's on the
record and it happened.

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:24.210
- My brother, whatever
else can be said of him,

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.930
can be quite clever.

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:28.210
Did an excellent job
of covering his tracks.

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:29.270
- Objection.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.670
The witness has admitted there's
no corroborating evidence

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:33.806
this supposed crime even occurred.

00:14:33.806 --> 00:14:36.175
- I would like to hear what he has to say.

00:14:36.175 --> 00:14:38.540
- Okay, this is called
the Speaking Objection,

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:39.920
and the problem with that is that

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:42.550
while some of the rationale
that she's using makes sense,

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:43.950
she's also not giving them a reason

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:44.810
under the rules of evidence,

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:46.810
why they cannot hear this evidence.

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.980
And the reason is it's pure speculation.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.350
He has admitted that he doesn't
have corroborating evidence.

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:52.610
You're not allowed to testify to that.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.797
And the judge, shouldn't be saying,

00:14:54.797 --> 00:14:55.870
"I want to hear what he says."

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:59.050
Now, again, this is something
that's like a bench trial,

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:00.390
something that's like an arbitration.

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:01.290
You see it all the time.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:03.040
They say, look, I'll hear the testimony.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.440
I understand that he might
be purely speculating,

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:06.790
but I want to hear that speculation.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:08.390
It might be based on something.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.530
And I am smart enough to know

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:13.070
whether I can trust that testimony or not.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:16.160
You see that a lot in
arbitrations or bench trials.

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:16.993
- All due respect,

00:15:16.993 --> 00:15:20.540
but you do sound somewhat
unhinged on the recording.

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:21.770
- Yes, I understand that,

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:25.880
but what you heard was theater,
a performance, play acting.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:29.300
I exaggerated the symptoms of my disease

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:30.851
to extract the truth.

00:15:30.851 --> 00:15:32.409
- I don't think that that's relevant here.

00:15:32.409 --> 00:15:36.020
They are kind of opening the
door to Jimmy and Kim here

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:37.960
to probe the veracity of it.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.670
That's a dangerous gamble.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.960
- Can we talk about your
disease for a moment?

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.400
I'd like everybody here to
be on the same page about it.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.490
- Of course, it is sometimes
referred to as EHS,

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.130
electromagnetic hypersensitivity.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:53.920
I describe it as an acute
allergy to electromagnetism.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:56.120
- It's not a common condition, is it?

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.480
- I know it sounds strange, I do.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.720
But 30 years ago, no one had
heard of peanut allergies.

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:03.500
- Okay, so this is really important

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:04.860
because generally in the law,

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:07.800
there is this idea that
you take your victim

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.110
as you find them.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.690
And often this is lumped in
with the Eggshell Skull theory

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:13.950
where let's say you punch someone

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:16.970
and they happen to suffer
from a bone disease

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:19.163
that gives them brittle bones,

00:16:20.163 --> 00:16:22.600
or a skull that is so thin
as to be like an eggshell.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:25.120
Well, the mere fact that you didn't intend

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:26.430
for some horrific injury to come

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:28.060
as a result of a mere punch,

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.640
given the special
circumstances of the victim,

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:31.473
it doesn't matter.

00:16:31.473 --> 00:16:33.120
You take your victim as you find them.

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.440
And that's really relevant
to the some things

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:36.811
that we're going to get to

00:16:36.811 --> 00:16:38.940
because Chuck believes
he has this disease.

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.940
And in some sense that matters,

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:41.897
whether that disease is true or not.

00:16:41.897 --> 00:16:43.130
And in other senses,

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:45.700
it matters only whether Chuck believes

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:47.170
he has this disease or not,

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:49.027
and his subjective reaction to it.

00:16:49.027 --> 00:16:51.720
That will become relevant
in just a second.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:52.720
- I love my brother.

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:55.773
There's nothing malicious in Jimmy.

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:00.000
He has a way of doing the worst things

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.243
for reasons that sound almost noble.

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:04.920
But what I know for sure,

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:08.060
is that the law is too
important to be toyed with.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.770
It's mankind's greatest achievement.

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:12.220
The rule of law, the idea
that no matter who you are,

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.100
your actions have consequences.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:15.480
- Preach, baby, preach!

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:16.470
- I have some questions.

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.540
Like the recorder, it must have hurt

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.330
like hell for you to touch that.

00:17:20.330 --> 00:17:22.710
- There was a degree of
discomfort, that's true.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:24.200
- And where did you hide it?

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:25.490
I mean, the sound's pretty clear

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:27.830
so it wasn't in the
couch cushions, was it?

00:17:27.830 --> 00:17:30.600
- Tucked under a space
blanket out of sight.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:33.270
- Wow, so there's no
chance I would see it.

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:35.870
Not with the state your place
was in, isn't that right?

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:36.703
- [Charles] I suppose.

00:17:36.703 --> 00:17:37.760
- Would you like to set the scene

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.410
for the disciplinary committee?

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:41.010
Tell them, you know, what your house

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:42.920
looked like at the time of the recording.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:44.580
- So, Jimmy's asking a combination

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:46.830
of open-ended and leading questions.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:48.732
And I remind you, that
on cross examination,

00:17:48.732 --> 00:17:51.060
you are allowed to ask leading questions.

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:52.720
It's only on direct examination

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.430
where you're not allowed
to ask leading questions.

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:56.910
- I covered most of the walls with

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.060
foil scrim craft insulation.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:01.420
I also hung a number of space blankets.

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:03.430
Compact Mylar sheets.

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:04.980
They insulate and protect against

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.390
mild electromagnetic radiation.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:10.800
- Right, so shiny insulation
and space blankets

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:12.580
all over the walls and the ceiling.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:15.120
It was like being inside of a disco ball.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:16.070
- Do you have a point?

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:18.800
- Okay, now Jimmy is
just flat out testifying.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.950
He's turns to the tribunal
and he just starts

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:22.800
saying what his own experience is like.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:24.220
You can't do that on cross examination.

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:25.105
He's a witness here.

00:18:25.105 --> 00:18:26.600
If he wants to take the stand

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.850
and testify as to what
he saw and experienced,

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:30.600
he's going to be able to do that.

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:32.410
- Usually, it's a perfectly normal house.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:33.660
- You think your house is normal.

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:35.893
Can I call your attention to exhibit nine?

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.000
This is your house, right?

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:43.833
- Yes.

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:46.483
- Exposed wires.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:50.043
There's a camp stove.

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:53.150
- Okay, so putting aside the fact

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:55.670
that Jimmy is basically
testifying right now,

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:58.890
I find this hilarious because every trial

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:00.490
has these exhibit binders.

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:01.810
And again, you're going to take care

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.610
of most of the evidentiary
stuff ahead of time,

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.600
so it's pretty normal
for all of the exhibits

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.120
to already be in evidence.

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:10.871
And we tend to carry these boxes

00:19:10.871 --> 00:19:13.560
of white three-ring binders,

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:16.190
or black three-ring binders
containing everything.

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:17.100
If he had planned this out,

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.990
he probably would have
blown up these pictures

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:21.320
on like a three by five
presentation board,

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:23.590
but spur of the moment
he wants to confront

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:25.950
the witness with these trial exhibits.

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:28.960
Totally spot on hilarious
the attention to detail here.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.210
And on top of that, yes,
he's sort of testifying,

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:33.870
but he would be able to
make the exact same points

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.777
by simply saying, "Is this your house?"

00:19:35.777 --> 00:19:37.860
"Do you recognize this
as a true and correct

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:40.160
picture of what was in your house?"

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:42.920
And, "Is it true as
depicted in this photo,

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:44.297
that there was exposed wire?"

00:19:44.297 --> 00:19:45.830
"Is it true that blah, blah, blah?"

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:47.286
You just simply confront the witness

00:19:47.286 --> 00:19:50.140
to verify all of these things so you get

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:51.830
the same effect of being able to show

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.090
the tribunal all of these photos.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:56.430
So, yeah, he's testifying
here, but in reality,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:58.300
what he would realistically
do with this witness

00:19:58.300 --> 00:19:59.440
is not that far off.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:00.730
It's great.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:03.610
- Right, so with the lights
out, you don't feel them?

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:05.750
- [Charles] If the
current's not flowing, no.

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:07.240
- Oh, sorry about the exit signs.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:09.450
I guess they couldn't kill those for you.

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:10.670
- Well, they're not drawing much current,

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:11.720
and they're far away.

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:15.000
Intensity drops off with distance

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.790
per the inverse square law.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:18.350
- Whoa, inverse square?

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:19.280
I'm not a physicist.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:21.760
Could you dumb that down a shade for me?

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:23.070
- The farther away it is,

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:26.020
the stronger the source needs
to be to have an effect.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:27.670
- All right, Huell is back.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:29.500
That'll become relevant in just a second.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:30.740
Now, things are about to get good.

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.440
Oh my god, I think I figured it out!

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:35.650
I can't wait to untangle this web again.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:37.050
I might've seen this before.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:38.863
- Got it, got it.

00:20:38.863 --> 00:20:40.220
So, if I had a small battery,

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:42.020
say from a watch or something,

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:42.880
and I got it close to you,

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:44.880
close to your skin, you'd know?

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.310
- [Charles] I would feel it, yes.

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:48.250
- Okay, laying the foundation that

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:51.050
a battery is something that he would feel

00:20:51.050 --> 00:20:52.230
and he would react to.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:53.560
- Can you feel more current coming

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.400
from any particular direction right now?

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:57.640
From that back wall?

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.080
Or from over there?

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:01.890
Or up through the floor?

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:05.193
Can you tell us where the
nearest source is right now?

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:12.200
- Jimmy, do you have
something in your pocket?

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:13.450
- No, he laid the trap!

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.030
He laid the trap!

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:17.593
- Yes, I do, as a matter of fact.

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:22.840
My cell phone.

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:25.050
From this distance, you should feel it,

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:26.610
and you don't, do you?

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:28.050
- Mr. McGill, you were warned to leave

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:29.870
your electronics outside.

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:30.830
- It's a trap!

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:31.920
It's a trap!

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:33.000
So, starting out here,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:34.550
I don't think that there's any issue

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:36.390
with him using his cell phone as a prop.

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:39.290
You use demonstratives
in trial all the time.

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:42.080
You know, you can generate testimony,

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:43.900
through the witness on the stand,

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:46.040
and the fact that it's his own cell phone,

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:47.300
it's unorthodox, sure,

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:49.080
but so is this medical ailment.

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:52.225
And in reality, you can
get a medical examination

00:21:52.225 --> 00:21:55.810
through discovery if this
was an actual court case.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:57.500
So, I think so far so good.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:21:59.270
This is akin to in "My Cousin Vinny",

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:01.990
when Joe Pesci is holding up two fingers

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:04.420
to test the eyesight of a witness.

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:06.500
- How many holding up now?

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:07.333
- Four.

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:11.200
- I don't think there's anything
untoward going on so far.

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:13.610
- What do I have to do to prove it to you?

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:14.710
- I don't know, Chuck.

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:17.460
Could you reach into your breast pocket

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:18.710
and tell me what's there?

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:21.933
- What now?

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:31.860
- [Jimmy] Can you tell
the court what that was?

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:33.770
- It's a battery!

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:35.204
It was on him the whole time.

00:22:35.204 --> 00:22:36.950
- Do you recognize that man in back?

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:38.400
His name is Huell Babineaux.

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:39.580
He's on our witness list.

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:41.300
You bumped into him in the stairway.

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:44.040
He'll testify he planted
this fully-charged battery

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.380
on you over an hour and a half ago.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:47.730
- [Huell] Hour and 43 minutes.

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:49.210
- [Jimmy] An hour and 43 minutes.

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:51.130
Thank you, Mr. Babineaux.

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:53.120
And you felt nothing.

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:55.070
- Here's where things
get really, really dicey.

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:55.903
Where do I start?

00:22:55.903 --> 00:22:56.940
I'm just, I'm just so excited.

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:58.080
There's so much stuff going on here.

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:00.560
First of all, the issue
of Jimmy testifying.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:01.470
Okay, yeah, all right.

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:03.590
So, Jimmy is sort of testifying here,

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:05.170
but I think what you would assume

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.410
would happen is that
the state would object.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.910
And what Jimmy's actually
doing is he isn't testifying

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:12.270
so much as he's making a proffer.

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:13.417
He actually says,

00:23:13.417 --> 00:23:14.735
"This is Huell Babineaux.

00:23:14.735 --> 00:23:16.510
He will testify."

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:18.720
He says something perspective in there.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.630
He's saying, I'm not offering
this into evidence right now,

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:22.770
but in the future,

00:23:22.770 --> 00:23:25.400
this person will testify to this,

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:26.770
and here's why it's relevant.

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:28.410
Lawyers do proffers all the time,

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:30.400
often in response to an
objection of relevance.

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:33.010
So yes, he is technically
testifying himself,

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.840
but he's only testifying into the things

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.260
that this witness, Huell Babineaux,

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:38.930
will testify to in the future.

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:41.771
And I'm sure if this disciplinary
hearing goes forward,

00:23:41.771 --> 00:23:44.580
he will make you Babineaux
take the stand and testify

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.070
to all the things that
have happened right now.

00:23:46.070 --> 00:23:48.290
So, it's conflating a
couple of things together,

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:49.800
but it's not that crazy.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:50.633
It's a proffer,

00:23:50.633 --> 00:23:53.840
but now is the secondary
issue of did Jimmy

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.690
commit a crime to elicit this testimony?

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:58.650
I think there's an argument that he did

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:01.250
because there is the crime of battery,

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:02.160
which is also a tort

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:04.772
where you engage in some
sort of harmful or offensive

00:24:04.772 --> 00:24:07.200
contact to another person.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.190
Now, harmful or offensive
doesn't just mean

00:24:09.190 --> 00:24:11.500
like punching someone or
stabbing them with a knife.

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:13.970
But it also includes things
that are singularly offensive

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:15.870
only to the victim themselves.

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:17.400
Contact that's highly offensive

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:19.990
to another's unusually sensitive sense,

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:22.520
and the actor knows that that contact

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:23.840
is highly offensive to them.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:25.170
So, when you're dealing with someone

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:27.110
that is incredibly unique,

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:29.210
the assailant has to know that

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:30.520
it's highly offensive to them.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:32.180
Arguably, that's the case here.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:34.510
Jimmy knows that Chuck feels this way,

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:36.660
regardless of whether it's true or not

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:38.850
that he actually suffers
from this condition.

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:41.390
So, in the restatement
of torts, for example,

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:43.610
they give a couple of
examples of things like this.

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:45.620
Serving pork to someone who doesn't eat

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:47.100
pork for religious reasons,

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:48.480
it's not going to hurt them,

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:50.150
but it might be highly offensive to them.

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:52.260
Or they give another example of someone

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:54.100
who is deathly afraid of butterflies.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:55.410
Well, the butterfly's
not going to hurt them,

00:24:55.410 --> 00:24:59.160
but if you put a butterfly on
someone and they freak out,

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:00.740
well, you've just committed battery

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:04.080
if you know that they are
terrified of butterflies.

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:05.150
And it generally doesn't matter

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:07.250
if the victim didn't know
about it ahead of time,

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:09.930
the damage is done when
they find out about it.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:12.540
The person who ate pork didn't
know that they ate pork,

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:13.640
but if you tell them about it,

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:16.140
well, then they have
suffered the emotional

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.790
distress of the battery,
even if it's after the fact.

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:21.361
On the other hand, there was an argument

00:25:21.361 --> 00:25:23.070
that this malady is not a real thing,

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:25.050
and therefore, he didn't suffer anything,

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:26.800
and a reasonable person
wouldn't suffer that either.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:27.633
I don't know.

00:25:27.633 --> 00:25:28.990
I could go either way on this.

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:30.710
And as for whether it's admissible

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:32.010
in front of this tribunal,

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:34.310
remember this is more
informal than a normal court,

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:36.370
but man, this one is still a tough one

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:39.736
because we don't want
defense attorneys accosting

00:25:39.736 --> 00:25:44.170
a material witness and
planting things on them

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:47.390
for what is obviously
highly probative evidence,

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:48.720
such a great scene.

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.020
I absolutely still love it,

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:52.400
regardless of whether
it would work or not.

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:54.197
It's just, it's just really good.

00:25:54.197 --> 00:25:55.030
(laughs)

00:25:55.030 --> 00:25:56.170
- You think this is bad.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:25:57.980
This, this chicanery.

00:25:57.980 --> 00:25:59.440
He's done worse.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:00.990
That billboard.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:02.270
Are you telling me that a man just

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:03.770
happens to fall like that?

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:06.583
No, he orchestrated it, Jimmy!

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:11.570
He defecated through a sun roof!

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:13.840
- Who doesn't love a
great Perry Mason moment?

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.532
This episode is just so good!

00:26:16.532 --> 00:26:19.115
(upbeat music)

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:21.890
So, now I have to give this episode

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:23.977
of "Better Call Saul" a
grade for legal realism.

00:26:23.977 --> 00:26:27.110
(gavel bangs)

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:30.000
On the one hand, they're
taking some liberties here.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:31.420
We've got people testifying.

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:33.760
You have people engaging in things

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.280
that normally wouldn't
be allowed in trial.

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:36.113
But on the other hand,

00:26:36.113 --> 00:26:37.170
this is a disciplinary hearing.

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:38.890
Things are going to be more informal

00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:41.270
and they really paid very close attention

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.970
to the actual ethical rules for lawyers

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:45.410
and things like FMLA.

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:47.600
And they were making
reasonably good objections.

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:51.090
Even though this caused me to
pull my hair out for months,

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:52.680
I think this is one of
the greatest episodes

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.740
of TV lawyering of all time,

00:26:54.740 --> 00:26:56.520
and I'm going to give it an A minus.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:26:58.340
Just don't engage in
this kind of chicanery

00:26:58.340 --> 00:26:59.600
as a real lawyer.

